window.docContents[173].resolve({"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Weak-Waves-and-Evolution/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Weak-Waves-and-Evolution--Strongly-Continuous-Semigroups--Nonlinear-evolution-from-a-convex-energy":{"contents":"A proper lower semicontinuous convex energy \non a real Hilbert space can also generate an evolution. Its subdifferential\nreplaces a linear generator:\n\n\n\nThe energy may be infinite outside its effective domain and need not be\ndifferentiable. The construction first solves the weak evolution for\ninitial values in . Contraction then\nextends the solution maps to , including initial values\nwhere a subgradient is not supplied.\n\nLet  be a real Hilbert space,  proper,\nlower semicontinuous, and convex, and\n. There is a continuous trajectory\n with  and a Bochner velocity\n such thatThe trajectory is unique in this continuous weak class, and the velocity\nis unique almost everywhere. Every  belongs to . If \nis the least-norm element of , thenNo density of  or separability of  is required.\n\nThe Yosida approximations have a common speed bound supplied by an initial\nsubgradient and converge uniformly on each bounded time interval.\nTheir limit has a weak velocity satisfying the subgradient inclusion;\ncountable exhaustion of the positive half-line gives one global velocity.\nChoosing the least-norm initial subgradient sharpens the speed bound.\nMonotonicity gives contraction between weak trajectories, hence uniqueness\nfor equal initial data; uniqueness of the weak derivative identifies their\nvelocities almost everywhere.\n\nLet  be a real Hilbert space and  be proper,\nlower semicontinuous, and convex. There is a unique jointly continuous\nflow  which is contractive and\nagrees with every weak subgradient trajectory starting in\n. In particular,For initial values in the closure the orbit is continuous and contractive;\nthe weak velocity and the subgradient inclusion are part of the\ndomain theorem for initial values\nin .\n\nThe global weak subgradient-flow theorem gives unique trajectories on\n. Monotonicity of the subdifferential makes the distance between two\ntrajectories nonincreasing. At each fixed time the solution map is\ntherefore -Lipschitz, so it extends uniquely from the dense set \nto the complete space .\nThe identity and composition laws pass to the closure by continuity.\nContraction controls changes of initial data uniformly in time, while\ncontinuity of each orbit controls changes of time; together they give\njoint continuity. Any other flow agreeing on  agrees everywhere\nby density.\n\nThe nonlinear Dirichlet diffusion\napplication identifies a concrete energy's subdifferential with a spatial\ndifferential operator. That identification is what turns this abstract\nevolution into a PDE with boundary data.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Weak Waves and Evolution\u0009Strongly Continuous Semigroups","header":"7.3.4.1. Nonlinear evolution from a convex energy","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Weak-Waves-and-Evolution/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Weak-Waves-and-Evolution--Strongly-Continuous-Semigroups--Nonlinear-evolution-from-a-convex-energy"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Representing-Solutions/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Analytic-Model-Methods--Power-series-and-analytic-model-methods":{"contents":"Power-series methods build a solution from its Taylor coefficients, and the\nfirst requirement is that the equation determine the normal derivative of\nthe unknown on the initial surface. For a first-order scalar equation\n, with  standing for the value of  and  for its\nderivative normal to the flat surface , that determination is an\nimplicit-function problem in the single variable . Once a system is in\nnormal form, the coupled analytic evolution theorem solves it.\n\nAn equation  is noncharacteristic in  at\n whenThis is the scalar implicit-function condition for the flat surface\n.\n\nNonvanishing of  at a point is precisely the invertibility\nhypothesis of the implicit-function theorem for the map\n, once the initial datum  is substituted\nfor . The step determines only the first normal derivative. Cauchy–Kovalevskaya goes further,\nsolving a recurrence for all higher normal derivatives and summing the\nresulting power series.\n\nAssume , the derivative in  is nonzero\nthere, and the composite  has positive\nregularity order  at . Then an open neighborhood of\n carries a graph  withthroughout that neighborhood, , and the same regularity order\nat . The theorem covers finite, smooth, and analytic orders. Choosing\nMathlib's analytic order  gives the analytic initial graph.\n\nThe scalar nonzero derivative gives the invertible partial derivative\nrequired by Mathlib's implicit-function theorem. Apply it to the composite\nat order . Its graph preserves the level ,\nwhich is zero by the base equation, and passes through the prescribed point.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Representing Solutions\u0009Explicit Representations and Transform Methods\u0009Analytic Model Methods","header":"9.2.1.8.1. Power-series and analytic model methods","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Representing-Solutions/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Analytic-Model-Methods--Power-series-and-analytic-model-methods"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Certified-Plots/#certified-plots":{"contents":"The Gaussian kernel, traveling sine, and Burgers profile have already served\nas examples of PDE identities. We return to them here to ask a different\nquestion: which values can each function take throughout a small spatial\ninterval? The enclosure machinery answers that question with a theorem for\neach panel. The horizontal axis is cut into forty\nrational panels, and a theorem places each function between two rational\nendpoints throughout every panel. The theorem is proved by\nthe cega tactic from an interval certificate and checked by Lean's kernel\nwhen the site is built. What is drawn is exactly the union of those certified\nboxes: three explicit solutions from the parabolic and hyperbolic chapters, as\nthe calculator certified them.\n\nHow to read a plot. Each colour is one value of the time parameter . Each\nshaded panel is one theorem: its horizontal extent is the panel  of\nthe -axis and its vertical extent is the certified range , so\nthe true curve runs inside the shaded strip. Each strip covers the function's\nvariation over the panel and may also include overestimation from the enclosure\ncalculation. Its endpoints are rounded outward to six decimal places so that\nthe statements stay readable.\n\nSelecting a panel opens its row in the theorem listing under the plot. Each\nrow gives the panel, the certified range, and the full Lean statement, of the\nform\n\nEnclosedOn (fun p : ℝ^1 ↦ Real.sin (p 0 - 1))\n  {p | 0 ≤ p 0 ∧ p 0 ≤ 3/20} (-841519/1000000) (-375639/500000)\n\n\nread \"for every point  of the one-dimensional Euclidean space with\n, the value  lies between the two\nrationals\". The theorem name links to its line in the generated module, which\nthe Blueprint build compiles. The generator asks the calculator for every\ncertified range before writing the theorems, and it checks that the drawing\nand the theorems list the same panels and bounds.\n\nThe first plot is the one-dimensional heat kernel\n\nat ,  and  on . Its total mass is one at\nevery time, so the curve spreads and flattens as  grows. The panels are\nnarrow strips near the tails, where the kernel is nearly flat, and taller\naround the flanks of the peak. The certificates handle the real power\n, the exponential and the constant  together; the\nGaussian-kernel section of the parabolic chapter proves the kernel's\nproperties as theorems and the textbook coverage map\nlocates them in Evans's §2.3.\n\n\n\nThe second plot is d'Alembert's traveling sine wave \nat ,  and  on . The wave moves to the\nright with unit speed, so the three curves are translates of one another by\none half. The function is the one whose wave-equation residual is checked in\nthe\nwave and transport example.\n\n\n\nThe third plot is the self-similar Burgers profile  at\n,  and  on . This is the formula used\ninside a rarefaction fan. The\nfull Riemann solution restricts\nit to the region between the rays determined by the two states and joins it\nto constant values outside. The plot encloses only the fan formula, on a\nfixed window, without the outer states. The\nprofile is increasing and linear in  on every panel, so its exact range is\ndetermined by its endpoint values. The strips show how the fan opens with\ntime: the same window of  covers a smaller range of velocities at later\ntimes.\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation","header":"10.12. Certified Plots","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Certified-Plots/#certified-plots"}});